ut i'm just i'm just saying  put it in perspective right only only  literally few weeks before party  conference  we announced what is i think an  extraordinary achievement by a prime  minister  ever since i joined the conservative  party successive governments labor and  conservatives  and they're never dealt with i  understand this but there is more than a  degree of frustration particularly  amongst people who are working looking  for work but on universal credit that he  didn't mention it it was as i mentioned  the single biggest cut to welfare that  we have seen in this country and he  couldn't be bothered to mention it as we  enter into colder winter with fuel  prices rising with food prices rising  you know and with well with jobs let's  just say for some people at a premium  and it's really important to remember  that  as we  arrived in manchester we announced half  a billion pounds  for winter for those families that will  find it most difficult on their fuel  bills on their  utility bills  according to the gather institute 840  000 people including almost 300 000  children are going to be pushed into  poverty as a result of the universal  credit uplift ending and he didn't  mention it i'm asking the question again  that i started with why didn't he why  did he choose to skirt the biggest  political issues of the day i'd i will  this is why i would respectfully  disagree because one we announced  literally as we were arriving in  manchester half a billion pounds for  those families for this winter on by the  way on top of the uh winter fuel  allowance the warm homes got all the  things for the most vulnerable families  to be able to help them over winter  months the  20 pounds uplift was always temporary  now  it was an important part of the 400  billion it was about 9 billion of the  400 billion that rishi sunak wrapped his  arms around the people who are most  vulnerable on the lowest pay many of  them on universal credit through the  pandemic but as we come out the other  end where we're seeing  jobs  market being incredibly dynamic there's  two million fewer  people out of work today than forecasts  through the probably four million  households with 3.3 million children  seeing their incomes drop by more than a  thousand pounds and the prime minister  can't be bothered to mention it the  reason i keep pushing on this point the  reason i keep pushing on this point is  it has felt at points during this party  conference that the conservatives have  been you know somewhat out of touch i  mean you had uh terese coffey you know  singing along you know on the at the  point at which the universal credit  uplift was removed singing along at a  karaoke event you've got another  colleague peter bottomley talking about  the grim struggle of living on an mp's  82 000 pounds a year salary i mean where  is the connection to people who are  struggling at the moment where is the we  had plenty of jokes from the prime  minister yesterday but where was he when  when some people wanted him to reach out  to the poorest  and some of the hardest working people  in this country and reassure them that  everything is going to be okay and he  glossed over it well  so where i'm  slightly hoping that as well as quoting  things like the 20 pound uplift you  would also quote the national living  wage has delivered four thousand pounds  more into people's uh pay packets uh  because of what boris johnson could have  made an announcement on raising minimum  wage yesterday  but that's done in you know there's a  there's a process to that indeed but you  didn't mention the half a billion which  we announced as we arrived in manchester  for those same vulnerable families that  will be delivered by their local  councils because they're the ones who  know them best for their for their fuel  bills for their utility bills and for  their food needs that's the same people  who may fall through the  safety net now  lots of other people  my job is to skill 